"""Original ecosocial systems and justice teaching."""
def course(c,m):
    return c("SWP-042","Ecosocial Systems and Environmental Justice","ecosocial-systems-and-environmental-justice","Ecosocial Systems\nand Environmental\nJustice",
        "Develop a justified environmental-justice analysis linking ecological conditions, everyday participation and accountable social work action.",[
m("Ecological dependence and everyday wellbeing","Trace a plausible pathway between an environmental condition and a person's participation without treating it as proven causation.",[
"Ecosocial practice considers how people depend on living environments as well as relationships and institutions. Water, land, air, food systems and shelter can influence everyday participation, while social arrangements affect who can use or protect these resources. Begin with a situation the person or community identifies as important. Ask what condition has changed, which activity is affected and what information supports the connection. A pathway diagram is a way to organise questions, not a scientific finding that one factor caused every reported difficulty. [R1, S1]",
"Distinguish a physical condition from the arrangements through which it matters. A water interruption may affect cooking, care, work or attendance differently across households. Storage, cost, mobility, tenancy and the reliability of another supply may change the practical effect. Record the person's account and identify what a relevant technical service must verify. Avoid assuming that one neighbourhood label describes every resident. The Port Harcourt study provides locally relevant contextual evidence, but its cross-sectional design cannot establish an individual household's cause or predict the effect of a new project. [R8]",
"Recognise ecological relationships without romanticising nature or a particular community. A green space may offer opportunities for social contact while its location, access rules or maintenance create barriers. Ask who can use it safely and whose livelihood or responsibilities make participation difficult. Do not prescribe an outdoor activity as a clinical treatment or assume that an environmental project automatically improves wellbeing. The learner's role is to identify a defensible practice question, involve the people affected and recognise when another profession's assessment is needed. [S1, R1]",
"The Finnish ecosocial study explored fourteen selected practitioners' views and organisational conditions. It offers questions about how a service understands environmental issues, rather than representative evidence of Nigerian practice or a test of a teaching method. Use that distinction in your analysis. Describe the proposed connection, the relevant evidence, an alternative explanation and a proportionate verification task. Include resources such as shared knowledge or mutual assistance alongside barriers. A useful analysis remains open to correction by the person and by appropriate specialist information. [R1, R8]"],
"Fictional case: residents report that an unreliable water point changes the time available for paid work and caring. Households have different storage and mobility arrangements. The worker records these differences and asks the authorised water service to verify the interruption before attributing every missed activity to it.",
"Write a five-link pathway from the reported water problem to one participation concern. Label each link as reported, observed or hypothetical, then give an alternative explanation and a verification owner.",
"A sound pathway keeps information status visible and recognises variation between households. It identifies a check and an appropriate role instead of diagnosing an environmental cause from one account.",["R1","S1","R8"],[
("Ecological and social conditions interact in everyday participation.","A water interruption is reported alongside missed work. What should an ecosocial analysis examine?","How the interruption, household arrangements and work conditions may connect.",["Only whether the person is motivated to work.","A guaranteed causal chain from the interruption to every absence.","The neighbourhood name as a complete household assessment."],"The task is to examine the connection and its evidence rather than assume a single explanation."),
("A pathway map organises hypotheses rather than proving them.","A learner draws a diagram connecting a local condition to an outcome. What does the diagram establish?","A proposed explanation whose links still need evidence.",["A causal effect equivalent to an intervention trial.","A diagnosis of everyone living nearby.","Proof that alternative explanations are irrelevant."],"Drawing a plausible relationship does not validate the relationship."),
("Similar environmental exposure can have different practical effects.","Two households use the same unreliable water point but report different difficulties. What should be checked?","Their relevant access, storage, mobility and daily responsibilities.",["Which household can be made to match the expected story.","Whether one account must be dishonest because the location is shared.","Only a community-wide average with no household context."],"Relevant capacities and circumstances can differ within the same setting."),
("An environmental activity is not automatically a clinical intervention.","A project offers time in a green space. Which claim stays within the introductory worker's evidence?","Its accessibility and intended participation purpose need checking with users.",["It treats every participant's health condition.","Its natural setting guarantees benefit for everyone.","No suitability or access review can be necessary."],"The worker should not infer clinical effectiveness from the setting."),
("Selected practitioner perspectives do not establish national prevalence.","How should the Finnish ecosocial study inform Nigerian service discussion?","As contextual questions to examine locally, with its selected sample stated.",["As the proportion of all Nigerian practitioners supporting ecosocial work.","As proof that a local project has improved outcomes.","As authority to replace residents' accounts with the study's themes."],"Qualitative views from a specific welfare setting need careful transfer."),
]),
m("Distribution voice and recognition","Examine who bears burdens, who influences a decision and whose knowledge is recognised.",[
"An environmental-justice analysis asks more than whether a project has an attractive overall aim. Examine how burdens and benefits are distributed, how people can influence the decision and whose experiences or identities receive attention. These are connected questions, not a mechanical score that decides a case. Start with the actual proposal and identify the people who may gain or lose access, time, livelihood or safety. Record the basis for each concern and avoid attributing motives to an organisation without evidence. [S1, S3]",
"Distribution concerns can remain hidden in a favourable average. A new facility may improve access for many while lengthening another group's journey or increasing an unaffordable charge. Ask what comparison is meaningful and whether the information can be collected safely and proportionately. Do not publish identifiable household details to demonstrate inequality. Where the evidence is incomplete, state whose experience has not been heard. A claim of fair distribution should explain both the benefits being considered and the burdens that remain unresolved. [R8, S1]",
"Procedural influence means knowing which decisions remain open and how contributions will be considered. A meeting held after a design is fixed offers a different opportunity from involvement before options are chosen. Ask who sets the agenda, controls relevant information and can authorise a change. The participation review examines power across climate initiatives and warns against equating participation language with meaningful influence. Its literature ends in 2020 and its thematic synthesis does not determine whether a particular local meeting is fair. Check the actual process. [R2]",
"Recognition involves taking people's knowledge and circumstances seriously without requiring them to present an identical account. Offer suitable ways to contribute and check whether language, timing, disability access or fear of consequences limits participation. A community leader's view can be valuable while leaving other perspectives unrepresented. Record disagreements respectfully and explain how a decision will be communicated back. The education standards support environmental justice and evidence-informed practice as learning domains; they do not certify that an analysis, project or academy has received external approval. [S3, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a shade project is placed beside a market entrance. Some traders welcome it, while evening workers say the chosen position obstructs their route. The team separates the distribution question from the decision process and invites affected users to examine options while a change remains possible.",
"Prepare a justice-analysis note with one distribution question, one influence question and one recognition question. Identify missing perspectives, relevant evidence and the role able to respond.",
"A strong note examines burdens and voice together, leaves disagreement visible and identifies a decision route. It does not treat attendance or a favourable average as proof of fairness.",["S1","S3","R8","R2"],[
("A favourable average can conceal uneven burdens.","Average travel time improves after a project but one group reports longer journeys. What should the analysis do?","Examine the distribution and the reported barrier with appropriate evidence.",["Discard the group's experience because the average improved.","Assume all benefits are equal across residents.","Publish private journeys with household names to prove the point."],"Distribution requires attention to differences as well as overall change."),
("Influence depends on decisions being open to change.","A consultation happens after the final design is approved. What needs to be made clear?","Which decisions, if any, participants can still influence.",["That attendance automatically constitutes co-design.","That a feedback form proves shared decision authority.","That no explanation of decision boundaries is needed."],"The opportunity to influence a decision should be described honestly."),
("Recognition does not require a single community narrative.","Residents give different accounts of a project's burden. What is an appropriate response?","Retain relevant differences and investigate them respectfully.",["Select one account and describe it as everyone's view.","Treat disagreement as proof that participation failed entirely.","Remove the less convenient account from the record."],"Differences can reveal circumstances that a single account would hide."),
("A representative's contribution does not demonstrate complete inclusion.","A community leader attends a meeting. What further participation question remains?","Whose perspectives and access needs may still be missing.",["Whether the leader can automatically consent for every household.","Whether other views can now be omitted.","Whether attendance proves every decision is fair."],"A useful contribution does not establish universal representation."),
("A curriculum benchmark does not approve a local justice analysis.","What does mapping a lesson to CSWE environmental-justice domains establish?","A learning-domain comparison with independent approval still separate.",["Accreditation of SWEEP without review.","A legally binding decision about a local project.","Equivalence to completing a professional degree."],"Educational mapping has a narrower purpose than accreditation or project approval."),
]),
m("Practice pathways and accountable advocacy","Convert an environmental-justice analysis into a bounded social work action and an evidence-aware advocacy statement.",[
"Choose a practice action that fits the analysis and the worker's authority. An immediate access concern may need individual support or an authorised referral. A recurring service barrier may need a discussion with the role responsible for the pathway. A contested policy may require a properly supported advocacy submission. These actions can connect, but one worker should not promise to resolve every ecological or institutional issue. Explain the intended contribution, the responsible role and what remains outside the service's authority. [S1, S3]",
"An advocacy statement should identify the decision being requested, the people affected, the relevant evidence and the uncertainty. Separate a reported concern from a verified technical finding. Do not allege contamination, a regulatory breach or event attribution from a visual impression alone. Ask an appropriate authority to assess the technical question while preserving the person's reported experience. Where a record includes personal information, use the approved process and share only what is justified for the purpose. Participation in an advocacy discussion is not unlimited permission to circulate someone's story. [S1, R8]",
"Select an action that can be followed through. For example, a service could clarify an inaccessible reporting route and name the role responsible for responding. Agree how people can learn what happened to the concern without exposing complainants. The environmental-justice curricular guide broadens the subject map across generalist practice; its older EPAS alignment is supplementary to the adopted 2022 standards. This course's cases and tasks are original and do not reproduce that guide's assignments. Benchmark use is not an accreditation claim. [S6, S3]",
"Review whether the promised action occurred and whether the access concern changed. A response letter, a completed meeting or a new sign is an output, not proof of improved wellbeing. Invite feedback that challenges the original analysis. If the action creates another burden, record it and use the authorised review route. The qualitative ecosocial study helps ask about organisational constraints, while the participation review helps examine influence. Neither establishes that this particular advocacy action caused a positive result. Keep the evidence proportionate to the conclusion. [R1, R2]"],
"Fictional case: residents find an environmental complaint route difficult to use. A worker helps them prepare a factual request for an accessible route and a named responder. Technical allegations remain for the appropriate authority to assess, and the update reports the response without naming individual complainants.",
"Draft a 150-word advocacy statement: requested decision, affected access, evidence status, responsible authority and review point. Add one sentence explaining what a completed response would not prove.",
"A defensible statement makes a specific request, protects relevant confidentiality and distinguishes social experience from technical findings. A recorded response is a step to review, not automatic evidence of impact.",["S1","S3","R8","S6","R1","R2"],[
("A practice action must match the worker's role and authority.","A community concern includes a possible technical pollution question. What can a generalist worker appropriately do?","Record the concern and seek assessment through the relevant authorised technical route.",["Certify contamination from appearance alone.","Promise a regulatory finding before an assessment.","Ignore the reported access concern until every scientific question is solved."],"Social support and appropriate technical referral can proceed within clear boundaries."),
("Advocacy distinguishes reports from verified findings.","Which wording best preserves evidence status in an advocacy note?","Residents report an interruption; the service is asked to verify its cause.",["The interruption proves intentional misconduct.","A report is the same as a confirmed technical cause.","Uncertainty should be removed to make the request stronger."],"Accurate attribution strengthens accountability without inventing findings."),
("Participation is not unlimited permission to publish a personal story.","A person contributes to a meeting about environmental access. What should govern later use of their details?","The agreed purpose and authorised information-use arrangements.",["Attendance as permission for every public campaign.","The worker's preference for a compelling story.","The assumption that advocacy removes confidentiality."],"Purpose and authority remain relevant when communicating publicly."),
("An advocacy output and a participation outcome are distinct.","A service sends a response letter. Which review question remains?","Whether the intended people can use the route and the relevant barrier changed.",["Whether a letter guarantees better wellbeing.","Whether further feedback is now unnecessary.","Whether every reported burden was automatically resolved."],"A completed communication is not the same as an improved experience."),
("New burdens should trigger a review of the action.","An access change creates an unexpected burden for another group. What should happen?","Record it, consult affected people and use the authorised adjustment process.",["Suppress the concern to preserve the success report.","Assume an environmental aim makes every consequence acceptable.","Treat the initial plan as permanently correct."],"Accountability includes revising an action when evidence challenges it."),
]),
])
